Overlay patch 0058: extend MK64's sky gradient beyond the 320x240 ortho
screen, so the VR pitch re-key (0044 rev7, VR R13 item 3) cannot expose its
edges.

MK64 draws its sky as two ortho quads in a guOrtho(0,320,0,240) screen: a
gradient from the top of the screen down to the horizon (screen->cameraHeight)
and, below the horizon, a floor quad down to y=0. Both END at the screen edge,
because in the flat game nothing can ever look past it.

0044 rev7 shifts that ortho projection vertically by the pitch difference
between the composed eye and the game's chase camera, which is what makes the
sky behave like a dome instead of a sticker. The moment it shifts, the quad's
own edge comes into view: look up far enough and the blue simply stops, with
whatever the eye framebuffer was cleared to beyond it.

The fix is a pair of CAP quads — one above the gradient, one below the floor —
each a flat extension in the colour of the edge it continues, so the gradient
itself is not stretched or re-ramped in any way. They are drawn between the
0046 sky sentinels, so they inherit the pitch shift with everything else and,
being part of the yaw-independent gradient half, they draw in the rear-view
pane too.

VR-only and __IOS__-guarded: gated on gSohVRWorldActive && gSohVRSkyMode, so
the flat game, the 3D panel and every non-world VR frame draw exactly what
they drew before. One shared static is enough — VR renders screen 0 only (no
split-screen in the headset).

rev2 (VR R14 item 1), two changes, both from the user's 1.0.0.10 device photos:

  * THE EXTENSION WAS FAR TOO SHORT. rev1 extended one screen height (240 ortho
    px) each way, which is a total sky of three screens. 0044 rev8 clamps the
    world-lock shift at +/-8 NDC = 960 ortho px, and looking up far enough with
    rev1's caps ran off the top of the upper cap and exposed the FLOOR quad and
    then its black lower cap: that is the "blue -> grey -> black" gradient
    filling the upper half of user-sky-grey-black-when-up.png. 2400 px each
    way covers the whole clamp with a screen of margin, which is what "the
    course's top sky colour all the way to the zenith" actually requires.
  * THE UPPER CAP NOW CONTINUES BOTH TOP CORNERS. rev1 copied vtx[0] (TopRight)
    onto all four cap vertices, so a course whose TopLeft and TopRight differ
    got a visible vertical seam where the cap met the gradient. Right-hand cap
    vertices take vtx[0], left-hand ones take vtx[3], and the floor cap takes
    vtx[5]/vtx[6] the same way — each cap vertex continues the gradient vertex
    directly below (or above) it, so the join is exact by construction.

rev4 (VR R17-A item 3) — THE CAPS GO SIDEWAYS, and the reason is trap D20.

the user's 1.0.0.13 rear-pane photograph (work/r17/user-rearpane-1.0.0.13.png)
has a BLACK VERTICAL STRIP at each edge of the pane, running from the pane's
top down to the wall/fence line — exactly the region the sky owns — with the
gradient present in the middle. It is the K = 120*AR - 160 family again:

    vtx[..].v.ob[0] = OTRGetRectDimensionFrom{Left,Right}Edge(...)

puts the gradient's edges at 160 -/+ 120*AR in the sky's guOrtho(0,320,...)
screen, where AR is `mCurDimensions.aspect_ratio` AT THE MOMENT THE DISPLAY
LIST IS BUILT. In VR that is the EYE extent's aspect (0044 deliberately leaves
mCurDimensions at eye size for the whole session), and the Vision Pro's eye is
5087x4081 = 1.246 — NARROWER than 4:3. So on device the gradient spans
x = 10.5 .. 309.5 of a 0..320 ortho: it does not reach the edges, and 3.3% of
the width at each side is left as clear colour. The eyes never showed it
because those columns land in the far periphery of the lens; the PANE is a
rectangle displayed edge to edge, so it shows it.

In THIS simulator the same eye is 1.778 (wider than 4:3), the gradient
OVERHANGS, and the defect is structurally invisible — which is why three rounds
of pane screenshots never caught it. `vr set skyarfault 1246` forces the
gradient's lateral extent to be computed at the device's aspect so the
simulator can photograph both arms of it (D57: the repro is the point).

The fix is the same shape as rev1's vertical caps: LATERAL cap quads that
continue the gradient's own edge colours outward, never a widening of the
gradient itself. Widening the quad would stretch its left-to-right colour ramp
and change the sky in the EYES on every course whose TopLeft and TopRight
differ; a cap adds pixels only where there were none. Four quads per side —
gradient, upper cap, floor, lower cap — each taking its colours from the
gradient vertex whose edge it continues, so every join is exact by
construction. The floor pair carry DrawFloor's 0x60/0x61 sentinels for the same
reason rev3's lower cap does. `vr set skywide 0` is the RED CONTROL and
restores 1.0.0.13 exactly.

It also unblocks R16-B step 4: `gSohVRSkyRoll` ships at 0 because "a 45-degree
roll pulls the screen corners to a radius of 200 ortho px against a quad 160 px
wide, so the gradient's left and right edges come into view". They no longer
do; the lateral extension is the same 2400 px as the vertical one. Turning the
roll on is still a separate decision and this round does not take it.

rev5 (VR R17-B) — THE SPRITE SPAN'S PROJECTION. Overlay 0051 rev7 turns the
cloud and star sprites into world-space billboards on a dome; this rev gives
DrawSkyActors the matrix state that needs, by re-issuing the GAME's own
perspectiveMatrix/lookAtMatrix pair (the Mtx GameCamera::SetViewProjection
loaded this frame) for the span and restoring the HUD ortho with the game's own
set_matrix_hud_screen() on the way out. It also settles R16-B's roll check: the
rev4 lateral caps mean the gradient now extends 2400 ortho px on ALL FOUR sides,
so a 45-degree roll -- which pulls a screen corner to a source point at radius
200 px -- cannot reach an edge, and gSohVRSkyRoll ships at 1.


